---
source: crates/surfaces/cli/src/main.rs
expression: command.clone().render_long_help().to_string()
---
GET a URL

Usage: specforge http get [OPTIONS] <URL>

Arguments:
  <URL>
          The URL, `https://` or `http://`

Options:
  -H, --header <NAME: VALUE>
          A request header, `Name: value` (repeatable); a credential comes from --secret

  -f, --field <KEY=VALUE>
          A string field of a JSON body, `key=value` (repeatable)

  -F, --field-json <KEY=JSON>
          A field of a JSON body given as JSON, `key=<json>` (repeatable): a number, a boolean, an array, an object

      --field-file <KEY=PATH>
          A string field of a JSON body read from a file in the project's roots, `key=<path>` (repeatable): a long text, such as a pull request's description, with no JSON escaping

      --body-file <PATH>
          The body, read from a file in the project's roots

      --secret <EXTENSION:NAME>
          A secret an extension declares, `<extension>:<name>` (e.g. `@gsd/core:github_token`), sent as `Authorization: Bearer <its value>` to the URL's host over https; its value is never printed or recorded

      --timeout <DURATION>
          Give up after this long: `10s`, `500ms`, `1m` (at most 60s)

      --max-bytes <BYTES>
          The most body bytes the answer keeps; past it, `truncated` is true
          
          [default: 1048576]

      --no-redirects
          Follow no redirect: a 3xx is the answer, with its `location` header

      --path <PATH>
          A path inside the project (the nearest directory at or above it holding specforge.json; none: the directory itself, with the default configuration)
          
          [default: .]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: json (the default) or human
          
          [default: json]
          [possible values: human, json]

      --pick <PATH>
          Print only the values at this path of the verb's JSON answer, one per line, e.g. '.entries[].path' on files list or '.branch' on git head. The path is steps: `.key`, `[n]` (digits, no leading zero), `[]` (every item) and `[<key>=<value>]` (the items whose field <key> is <value>, e.g. `[kind=plan]`: <key> may be dotted, `[fields.result=issue]`, and a number, boolean or null is compared as JSON, `[fields.backlog=true]`, `[fields.wave=2]`; an object or array is never selected); a `.` before `[` may be left out, and `.` alone is the whole answer. One line per value reached: a string bare (a newline in it is printed as is), an integer as is and any other number as an f64 (`1.0`, `-0.0`, `1e3` as `1000.0`), a boolean, object or array as compact JSON; null or a missing value prints nothing, and is not an error. It takes no --format human: errors print as they do under json, and the exit code is the verb's.

      --count
          With --pick: print the number of values the path reaches, the lines --pick would print (null or a missing value is none, a string holding a newline is one), 0 for none; nothing when the command answered nothing, so `$(… --count) || n='?'` tells an unread answer from 0. It takes no --first, --last, --join or --fields (ADR 0325 D8).

      --first <N>
          With --pick: keep only the first N values it reaches

      --last <N>
          With --pick: keep only the last N values it reaches

      --join <SEP>
          With --pick: print the values kept on one line joined by SEP exactly as given (no escape is read: $'\n  ' is the shell's), then a newline; nothing when no value is reached. It combines with --first or --last, and takes no --count or --fields (ADR 0325 D8).

      --effects <EFFECTS>
          The effects mode: online (the default; auto is online), cached or replay. http sends the request, answers a GET from a fresh record of the effects journal (cached), or answers from the journal alone (replay); files and git read and write this machine's disk in every mode, and the journal keeps none of it

          Possible values:
          - online: Performed when granted; the answer is journaled
          - auto:   A fresh record; else performed when approved; else not performed
          - cached: A fresh record; else not performed
          - replay: Any record, expired or not; else a miss

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')
